# How reading works

The server downloads your sources **in your order**, measures how long each one is, and assigns reading agents until a reading budget of about {{ (budget / 1000) | int }}k tokens is used, at most {{ max_papers }} papers. A typical paper is 8k–15k tokens; long ones with appendices reach 35k.
- The **first** source is always read completely. Put the core paper there.
- The following ones are read completely while the budget lasts, then only their key sections, then not at all.
- So order strictly by how indispensable a source is for the key questions. Listing more than fit is fine: they are the reserve if a download fails.

# Your task

- Set the focus of the episode (in English): one clear common thread rather than a grab bag.
- Rank up to {{ count }} sources that together answer the key questions: the core paper(s), plus context, critique or follow-up work. The scout score is only a hint; pick from further down the list when the content fits. Avoid near-duplicates (e.g. a preprint and its journal version).
- Prefer sources with a full text (arXiv ID, PDF link or DOI).
- Use the IDs exactly as listed. Write each `reason` in English, one sentence.
